G2 — the legacy wire caches across a caught inline failure: proposed fix
=========================================================================

NOT APPLIED, NOT COMPILED. Unified diff against Sparkle/Compiler/Elab.lean at
f91da85a (`patch -p1 --dry-run < docs/parallel/G2-cache-fix.patch` from the
repository root). Text before the first `---` line is ignored by `patch`.

Defect (Tests/Compiler/LegacyCacheRollbackTest.lean, run on cab870c0):
`Rec.tryInlineUnfold` catches a failed first attempt; the `catch` restores the
builder state (wires, `usedNames`, the name counter) but not the `IO.Ref`
caches the attempt wrote. An entry left behind names a wire that no longer
exists (`parentRetry`: `_tmp_op_a_0` read, never declared) or that the
rolled-back counter gives to another expression (`parentRealloc`: a
well-formed module, out = 23 where the source is 19 at x = 5).

VARIANT A (the diff below) — restore the caches on the catch paths
------------------------------------------------------------------
`snapshotWireCaches` before the attempt, `restoreWireCaches` at the head of
both `catch` branches of `Rec.tryInlineUnfold`. Restored: the expression
cache of the context, `sparkleLetWireCache`, `sparkleLoopWireCache`,
`sparkleWireCanon`, `sparkleSingleOutInstanceCache`,
`sparkleSubInstanceOutputs`, `sparkleWireWidthCache`, `sparkleFvarValueMap`,
and `sparkleFvarZetaVisited` (a second leak of the same kind: the zeta arm
inserts the fvar, translates, erases — with no `finally`, so a failure under
it leaves the fvar marked and every later reference to it throws "circular
zeta-reduction"). Not restored: `sparkleSubModuleCache`, `sparkleChildCache`
(module results; the child-cache invariant of `childKeeps` is about them and
is untouched), the type cache, the counters.

It is the only `catch` in `CompilerM` that goes on TRANSLATING after a failed
translation: the other `try … catch` of the translator wrap a type query, a
literal extraction or a child's synthesis in `MetaM`, none of which writes
these caches for the parent's module and then continues in the parent.

Sources that compile today:
* a compile in which no inline attempt fails: byte-identical output — the
  restore never runs, and the snapshot only reads;
* a compile in which an attempt fails and is recovered from: the recovery
  re-lowers what the failed attempt had cached, instead of reusing its names.
  Output differs exactly where today's output refers to a rolled-back wire.
  No natural source of this kind is known (docs/parallel/G2-legacy.md §11;
  Tests/Compiler/LegacyCacheNaturalTest.lean), so on the corpus the expected
  diff is empty;
* the accepted set: a recovery that today is REJECTED by `instLinkCheck`
  because a stale argument wire is undeclared (`parentSub` with the injected
  fault) compiles after the fix. Nothing that compiles today is rejected.

Cost: a snapshot keeps a second reference to each map, so the FIRST insert
into each map after it copies the map once (`Std.HashMap` is updated in place
only when unshared). That is O(size of the caches) per inline attempt of an
untagged helper call — on every such call, failing or not. The shim's own
comment (Elab.lean 7863–7869) records that exactly this sharing made a
large FSM quadratic. If a timing measurement shows it, the snapshot has to be made cheaper (not replaced by clearing: see A').

Proofs to redo: `tryInlineUnfold_tripleC` (Tools/ShippingChildLegacy.lean)
and `tryInlineUnfold_ensures` (Tools/ShippingLegacySemInline.lean) gain the
snapshot bind and one restore per handler; the reads and writes are already
in the frame class (`Framed.sideGetIO` / `sideSetIO` for the eight global
refs — all are `SideRef`s — and `localGetIO` / `localSetIO` for the context's
cache). `tryInlineUnfold_returns`' statement is unchanged.
`InlineUnfoldRegressionTest` compares against the old cell on scripted
translators: unaffected (same calls, same results).

VARIANT A' — clear instead of restore (NOT PROPOSED; withdrawn)
---------------------------------------------------------------
An earlier version of this header offered "set the same refs to empty in
both `catch` branches" as a cheaper alternative and called its effect "same
function". That claim is NOT established and the variant is withdrawn: after
a recovered failure it throws away sharing built up BEFORE the attempt, so a
later call equal to an earlier one gets its own instance (Issue #107 /
Issue #71) and a later evaluation of the same `Signal.loop` gets its own
registers. Whether two copies of a stateful instance or of a loop's
registers behave like the one shared copy is a property of the design's
state sharing, not something this patch may assume. Variant A is the fix.

VARIANT B — validate the hit (docs/parallel/G2-legacy.md §9.1; not in the diff)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The shim honours a hit only if the pure builder state records the wire for
the expression (`cacheLookupValidated` / `recordTranslation`). It removes the
stale hit of the EXPRESSION cache at no cost on the success path and is what
the proofs need (`ShimAt` for every call), but it does not cover the hash-keyed
caches (let / loop / instance), which need A, A' or a move into `CircuitState`
(§9.2), and it changes hits that are not stale (`mdata`-stripped keys,
results that are existing wires), so its corpus diff is not expected to be
empty. A fixes the defect; B is for the proof and is independent.

--- a/Sparkle/Compiler/Elab.lean
+++ b/Sparkle/Compiler/Elab.lean
@@ -5566,6 +5566,61 @@
     | _ => ex
   | ex => ex
 
+/-- The `IO.Ref` caches whose entries NAME WIRES of the module being built
+    (or mark work in progress on it), read together.  A `catch` in `CompilerM`
+    restores the builder state — wires, `usedNames`, the name counter — but
+    not these refs: an entry written by the failed attempt would name a wire
+    that no longer exists, or that the rolled-back counter hands out again to
+    another expression (Tests/Compiler/LegacyCacheRollbackTest.lean).  So a
+    `catch` that goes on translating has to put them back as well.
+
+    Not included: `sparkleSubModuleCache` / `sparkleChildCache` (module
+    results, valid whatever the parent's builder state), `sparkleTypeCache`
+    and the profile counters. -/
+structure WireCaches where
+  expr : Option (Lean.ExprStructMap String)
+  letWire : Std.HashMap String String
+  loopWire : Std.HashMap String String
+  wireCanon : Std.HashMap String String
+  singleOut : Std.HashMap String String
+  subInstOut : Std.HashMap (UInt64 × String) String
+  wireWidth : Std.HashMap String Nat
+  fvarValue : Std.HashMap Lean.Name Lean.Expr
+  zetaVisited : Std.HashSet Lean.Name
+
+/-- Read the wire-naming caches. -/
+def snapshotWireCaches : CompilerM WireCaches := do
+  let expr ←
+    match (← CompilerM.getCompilerState).exprCache with
+    | some ref => do
+      let m ← CompilerM.liftMetaM (ref.get : IO _)
+      pure (some m)
+    | none => pure none
+  let letWire ← CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleLetWireCache.get : IO _)
+  let loopWire ← CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleLoopWireCache.get : IO _)
+  let wireCanon ← CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleWireCanon.get : IO _)
+  let singleOut ← CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleSingleOutInstanceCache.get : IO _)
+  let subInstOut ← CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleSubInstanceOutputs.get : IO _)
+  let wireWidth ← CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleWireWidthCache.get : IO _)
+  let fvarValue ← CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleFvarValueMap.get : IO _)
+  let zetaVisited ← CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleFvarZetaVisited.get : IO _)
+  return { expr, letWire, loopWire, wireCanon, singleOut, subInstOut, wireWidth,
+           fvarValue, zetaVisited }
+
+/-- Write the wire-naming caches back. -/
+def restoreWireCaches (c : WireCaches) : CompilerM Unit := do
+  match (← CompilerM.getCompilerState).exprCache, c.expr with
+  | some ref, some m => CompilerM.liftMetaM (ref.set m : IO Unit)
+  | _, _ => pure ()
+  CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleLetWireCache.set c.letWire : IO Unit)
+  CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleLoopWireCache.set c.loopWire : IO Unit)
+  CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleWireCanon.set c.wireCanon : IO Unit)
+  CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleSingleOutInstanceCache.set c.singleOut : IO Unit)
+  CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleSubInstanceOutputs.set c.subInstOut : IO Unit)
+  CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleWireWidthCache.set c.wireWidth : IO Unit)
+  CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleFvarValueMap.set c.fvarValue : IO Unit)
+  CompilerM.liftMetaM (sparkleFvarZetaVisited.set c.zetaVisited : IO Unit)
+
 section TranslatorBlock
 variable (translateExprToWire : (e : Lean.Expr) → (hint : String := "wire") →
   (isTopLevel : Bool := false) → (isNamed : Bool := false) → CompilerM String)
@@ -6216,10 +6271,14 @@
       | some e' => return e'
       | none => return e
   if eReduced != e then
+    -- The catches below restore the builder state; `saved` lets them restore
+    -- the wire-naming `IO.Ref` caches too (see `WireCaches`).
+    let saved ← snapshotWireCaches
     try
       let w ← translateExprToWire eReduced hint (isNamed := isNamed)
       pure (some w, none)
     catch ex1 =>
+      restoreWireCaches saved
       -- Inline expansion failed (often due to mixed Signal/BitVec operators
       -- inside the expanded body). Retry with reducible transparency to
       -- prevent over-expansion of Signal.pure and OfNat instances.
@@ -6235,6 +6294,7 @@
         else
           pure (none, some ex1)
       catch ex2 =>
+        restoreWireCaches saved
         pure (none, some ex2)
   else
